#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b70db523-cb7c-5e94-b140-85f0bfe26723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b70db523-cb7c-5e94-b140-85f0bfe26723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316158} :answer-field/id #uuid "da810c64-2e96-5474-bd2f-f95d41c06a27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3cd8be64-dc7a-5f8f-aeb5-0d33c74cf476" :question/math-academy-id "q-344221" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{(-1)^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fac581ea-573a-58c1-b5bf-d867db6529a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1275}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fac581ea-573a-58c1-b5bf-d867db6529a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1275}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316522} :answer-field/id #uuid "3820224b-6d56-57e5-aac2-f726f0b03ec6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eec707f8-4b36-53bb-89b3-e76de724d729" :question/math-academy-id "q-344298" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{32}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{32}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{32}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{32}{x}^{3/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/5+1}}{(\\frac{3}{5}+1)}{|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & ={\\frac{5}{8}\\,{x}^{8/5}|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & ={\\frac{5}{8}\\,(\\sqrt[5]{x})^{8}|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\,((\\sqrt[5]{32})^{8}-(\\sqrt[5]{1})^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\,({2}^{8}-{1}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}(256-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1275}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125b0a7c-e2d8-53b2-b759-39f49f437bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{508}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "125b0a7c-e2d8-53b2-b759-39f49f437bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{508}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315227} :answer-field/id #uuid "27070b9f-fbf4-5c0b-ad12-fce7452ab824" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7f92646-9b6e-589d-9144-c41fa322b042" :question/math-academy-id "q-344299" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{16}\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{16}\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{16}{x}^{3/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/4+1}}{\\frac{3}{4}+1}{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{7/4}{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}{(\\sqrt[4]{x})}^{7}{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}({(\\sqrt[4]{16})}^{7}-{(\\sqrt[4]{1})}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}({2}^{7}-{1}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}(128-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{508}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317507 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6ee50ad-f5e6-5ccc-8f77-df111921b3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(2∗{2}^{1/3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6ee50ad-f5e6-5ccc-8f77-df111921b3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(2∗{2}^{1/3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316318} :answer-field/id #uuid "151c7883-0bac-5441-a408-c45dbb1501af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "36e19ea4-7147-564a-aefc-cb94da408789" :question/math-academy-id "q-344503" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{16}{x}^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-2/3+1}}{(-\\frac{2}{3}+1)}{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =3\\,{x}^{1/3}{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =3\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt[3]{16}-\\sqrt[3]{1}) \\\\  & =3(2\\sqrt[3]{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f71379ca-dfb4-5d56-adba-c3c3f3d771b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f71379ca-dfb4-5d56-adba-c3c3f3d771b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318382} :answer-field/id #uuid "4287acad-245f-58bd-be4f-6dbce3ede11e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9181afbc-9550-51c6-b28c-f82450210472" :question/math-academy-id "q-348109" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}+1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(2)^{3}-4(2)=8-8=0 \\\\ y & =2(2)^{2}+1=2(4)+1=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,9).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-4,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4t}{3{t}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{4(2)}{3(2)^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3(4)-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{12-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the Cartesian equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,9)$ and the slope $m=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-9 & =x \\\\ y & =x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the tangent line  in slope-intercept form is $y=x+9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a97d047-8132-5364-9050-62b56b8464f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a97d047-8132-5364-9050-62b56b8464f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317873} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bbbd214-a055-53f2-b3b5-67bd9aed6a47" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318484} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd25ab4-278a-5d16-bdb6-ac73bab524da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bd25ab4-278a-5d16-bdb6-ac73bab524da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317877} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee152229-0df5-5943-83fa-bcee1ecff76b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a109daf-d742-5b15-87da-27961e643b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348115" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2),\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=e$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=e$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)=3e \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(3e,1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=e,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,e} & =\\frac{1}{e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3e,1)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{1}{4e}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1=\\frac{1}{4e}(x-3e).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{1}{4e}(0-3e) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,\\frac{1}{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "837b7697-38dc-5ec2-a5e1-0f7eef16059a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "837b7697-38dc-5ec2-a5e1-0f7eef16059a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318009} :answer-field/id #uuid "35e83485-026d-51c3-a54b-33ed9e867b92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a64ef463-d187-5f34-bc23-8e25a68dd5a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-348303" :question/problem "![](images/59/59f053b9a122018d78cd9c30f5a53cd67cd2ff645d02ccb6d311e6183e081102.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}+2{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2t}{3{t}^{2}+4t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3t+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=2,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{2}{3(2)+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70131d8e-e110-5e84-bcff-45651751b52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70131d8e-e110-5e84-bcff-45651751b52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317953} :answer-field/id #uuid "805f7f24-9fb6-5e8f-8459-868c76a93452" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab4d806a-76c8-563a-a10d-c7fabb39c7b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-348304" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7ae65c873838ede4cce89c953ff2654fa1e58e65fdc87896c8ba85bbf947be20.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12952b5e-889f-54cc-a122-5d50215660f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12952b5e-889f-54cc-a122-5d50215660f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317625} :answer-field/id #uuid "162eb28c-9915-5e71-854b-15570d6140d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc186f89-e80b-5eb4-a0f9-50d2a3804e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc186f89-e80b-5eb4-a0f9-50d2a3804e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318301} :answer-field/id #uuid "45248884-e4ee-5dd3-b87d-9707883c9d1c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ca39289-4112-5812-a40f-16d38e719123" :question/math-academy-id "q-350359" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}+14t+8 & =0 \\\\ (3t+2)(t+4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-4) & =2(-4)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{15}{2}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right) & =2\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{6}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-4$ and $t=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e270e8d4-fbf0-5120-8d17-e3b700621933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e270e8d4-fbf0-5120-8d17-e3b700621933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318320} :answer-field/id #uuid "57214c85-b840-55fd-b641-4b1c74936c23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318612} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23a8be49-ec43-55d3-af29-85069b1bbe66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23a8be49-ec43-55d3-af29-85069b1bbe66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317677} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a3120f3-3da1-51f8-aa50-545e1dd0fd1e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fa66657-dfb2-58cb-a875-a940d0ba53d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-350368" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)=-1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9706bfa8-3bb2-557b-b07a-3cb1417d9a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9706bfa8-3bb2-557b-b07a-3cb1417d9a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318080} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c918410-e361-5bcd-9d06-8fb2ed574022" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318509} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9f0baf7-6810-54d5-a562-b853fd4577c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9f0baf7-6810-54d5-a562-b853fd4577c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318344} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d59423d-64dc-523d-9ab7-74063ac68307" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12a180e2-5235-5f82-b761-d705d49812b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-350386" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}=1-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}+7(-1)^{2}+8(-1)=-1+7-8=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(1,-2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}+14t+8}{2t+\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}+14(-1)+8}{2(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,-2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal intersects the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) & =2 \\\\ x-1 & =-4 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(-3,\\,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a237665-5e72-5ef0-972a-daed0f365535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a237665-5e72-5ef0-972a-daed0f365535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318093} :answer-field/id #uuid "429e4c9a-9cfb-50e6-a993-4807d39b6109" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318565} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24461eb9-a35c-56e6-b177-bda2a062fa02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24461eb9-a35c-56e6-b177-bda2a062fa02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317678} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e11efaf-e900-5155-8422-a40a0e3088ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe83c43c-ad52-5fa5-817f-abc0952cf819" :question/math-academy-id "q-350395" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7{t}^{2}-9t,\\,y={t}^{6}+2{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7({1}^{2})-9(1)=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{6}+2({1}^{2})=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,3).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=14t-9,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{5}+4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{5}+4t}{14t-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{6({1}^{5})+4(1)}{14(1)-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,3)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dcd11c4-604f-5bca-bd31-a170bd84a811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dcd11c4-604f-5bca-bd31-a170bd84a811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317946} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec3bc6ba-dba0-565a-9d3e-6d25c11cdc38" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d9076ce-d69d-5ee0-95b8-de3533c91e39" :question/math-academy-id "q-350403" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-8t,\\,y=2{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe normal to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t-8,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-8 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =8 \\\\ t & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(4) & =6{(4)}^{2}=96\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dccbdf4-6b20-5048-8475-7edb8b5f47ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dccbdf4-6b20-5048-8475-7edb8b5f47ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317614} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8a6d76-f112-5663-9241-788ae415e493" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095b8b92-1a17-5015-a26c-15163e676f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "095b8b92-1a17-5015-a26c-15163e676f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317600} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb60be2-41b3-5c75-b56a-529c11e5a236" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0473c92-6bbb-51d0-9d33-3793374dc209" :question/math-academy-id "q-350414" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+8,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-5,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $t=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9dd0123-b8e1-55f5-8f39-55cc94febb3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9dd0123-b8e1-55f5-8f39-55cc94febb3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319902} :answer-field/id #uuid "079d2083-b887-5746-8f4a-2763a37f8c60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48e499b2-6d1b-5fe4-a033-41b4b4365949" :question/math-academy-id "q-353340" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0b1dc90950ba127e90fce400bc7ed1b6e8dd8e710ff880ab5949521b02a258a.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=4{e}^{-t},\\,y={t}^{2}+6t,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4{e}^{-t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t+6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t+6}{(-4{e}^{-t})}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{2(1)+6}{(-4{e}^{-1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8e}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0f10ff0-18c6-5914-a313-73bb4cb93ff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0f10ff0-18c6-5914-a313-73bb4cb93ff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319929} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c2d3244-6f8c-503e-84d8-0399c320617e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d193d39c-471b-5fec-8b86-474bbc1fb6ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-353341" :question/problem "![](images/18/1817c65aea37112d93f2935801a0e7dcd654706a72f729810272f48e95d39665.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /6}=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{(-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57e66287-df20-5352-8c57-82613f6d9872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57e66287-df20-5352-8c57-82613f6d9872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319651} :answer-field/id #uuid "47d2aa81-56a9-5ec0-8780-da583c96885a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0af90cd-0dda-500f-9fc6-6a569abe78d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-353343" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y=3{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=2.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{6t}{2{e}^{2t}}=\\frac{3t}{{e}^{2t}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2}{{e}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{e}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77052cc4-8eea-5cef-8b05-26c9c4eef0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77052cc4-8eea-5cef-8b05-26c9c4eef0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319748} :answer-field/id #uuid "85e642b6-7d86-530e-bd66-8c4ef03c3bbc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c960a70d-cb11-51ca-88d4-50c4fa7900ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-353344" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=5t-3,\\,y={t}^{4}+2t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=5,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}+2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4{t}^{3}+2}{5}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{4(1)^{3}+2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fab36b16-5bde-52a2-af10-016c5c78e4ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fab36b16-5bde-52a2-af10-016c5c78e4ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320145} :answer-field/id #uuid "3297beab-a596-5653-8a3c-f59721811ee1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1a4b6d5-bbe2-5c0f-8f42-413948326feb" :question/math-academy-id "q-353346" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t},\\,y=8t-3,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=4.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,4}=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{8}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\right)}=16\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=4$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,4} & =16\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =16(2) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7c3b133-15d8-5457-8b33-856a0f15034b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7c3b133-15d8-5457-8b33-856a0f15034b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319891} :answer-field/id #uuid "fee9d754-1e3f-5127-b952-ef485c0de56c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7ab1fc4-f7f2-55b9-a74c-b824a475ac09" :question/math-academy-id "q-353347" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{3t},\\,y=2{e}^{t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{3{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-2t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56511671-1b56-5091-a324-56b442d1f811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56511671-1b56-5091-a324-56b442d1f811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319644} :answer-field/id #uuid "88549d85-6690-5559-9bdd-e3b23c490bbc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88d2c31a-1162-5af9-962c-a704ed1fd8ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-353350" :question/problem "![](images/ab/abab926e465522d30b236577bf8a1654502807b864c9fed8a4cebb84efffd3a2.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397cc35d-3f47-519e-b5bf-73d3a8b311b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "397cc35d-3f47-519e-b5bf-73d3a8b311b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319568} :answer-field/id #uuid "6920c4ef-fcdf-5331-9ff1-0d0ddf66b7c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6474a49e-c58a-508a-9e50-a787079f364a" :question/math-academy-id "q-353351" :question/problem "![](images/35/35c62ccf48c4d0a49729da04b443cb94d112e673e0c24e7784d22f48eaabf8f7.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /3} & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acc15cc8-16fd-50fa-8523-a77c71d90858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acc15cc8-16fd-50fa-8523-a77c71d90858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322500} :answer-field/id #uuid "2032fb96-09c8-5ded-b5bd-deeda3577ddf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323229} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d38fff03-6da9-5ce6-a633-7885919027e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d38fff03-6da9-5ce6-a633-7885919027e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322799} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0b201f2-c9f2-5028-a916-abafa9097eaa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "587316a5-f847-5f83-912c-b52ebafdaeb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-357255" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a52de76-716b-5d41-98a9-3943b7c59241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a52de76-716b-5d41-98a9-3943b7c59241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321352} :answer-field/id #uuid "abecd077-f709-5b82-9c40-6c4dc262a11d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae92af54-aa04-5ea8-bfcd-575703012570" :question/math-academy-id "q-363490" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{3/t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}},\\,0<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\right)}{(-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3/t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3({1}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(5)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15\\sqrt{5}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4018c0-04da-53c3-b1b1-95d97650b6c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321351} {:answer/id #uuid "21ebd7a8-633f-510a-b7a0-122610bd643d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}-{x}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321403} {:answer/id #uuid "335c4565-497d-5a35-8a36-3ece522afdc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm (y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321537} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f235af-0ac8-5f53-9eb1-bed01e2c9022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322423} {:answer/id #uuid "aa38752c-037f-5141-b1e6-be7e8674acb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1f235af-0ac8-5f53-9eb1-bed01e2c9022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322423} :answer-field/id #uuid "0203c359-4ecf-5220-b7ce-6106cd0e6714" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8437fb94-196b-50e1-ad78-1cd44475ec42" :question/math-academy-id "q-36825" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $Q(1,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $Q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =\\frac{2(1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $Q(1,1)$ is the $1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 2y & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $Q(1,1)$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9a8991-24fd-50f9-8941-a329e3133c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{s}^{4}{t}^{2}+10{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321230} {:answer/id #uuid "84b790fb-dce2-5ea5-9fbd-fb66967b78c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{s}^{2}{t}^{4}-20s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322195} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1f027a-f67b-5d1f-b407-9ee3b85b3667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{s}^{4}t-5{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322598} {:answer/id #uuid "c8bfc905-fde6-54a5-83ad-c5fa1e325d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322709} {:answer/id #uuid "f675ee7a-8c5d-5a9a-90cb-d0e4dd971fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{s}^{2}{t}^{4}-15s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8bfc905-fde6-54a5-83ad-c5fa1e325d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322709} :answer-field/id #uuid "af1de9ae-38d9-5d66-a275-9fc76c319cbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd5b5db6-0961-5b1c-8f50-24c2f4348bc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37132" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y-5yz,$ where $x=st,$ $y={s}^{3}$ and $z=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}-5z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(st)({s}^{3})=2{s}^{4}t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(st)^{2}-5(s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)={s}^{2}{t}^{2}-5s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =-5{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =3{s}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(2{s}^{4}t)\\cdot t+({s}^{2}{t}^{2}-5s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3{s}^{2}+(-5{s}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}+3{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-15{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043fc35b-df2d-5e07-8cbe-03b7fa3823c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321189} {:answer/id #uuid "518cc1b5-633b-5f44-b41f-79ef11a5b400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-8{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321789} {:answer/id #uuid "704123a5-c11a-5ff9-85e0-48be32c71cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{s}^{5}{t}^{2}+2{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322021} {:answer/id #uuid "cbea4778-d1ae-52ee-b74f-3fd686a2645b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{s}^{3}{t}^{2}-4{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322731} {:answer/id #uuid "e322d4a9-1941-524c-a4fe-437235ac0079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-5{s}^{2}+2{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "518cc1b5-633b-5f44-b41f-79ef11a5b400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-8{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321789} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1da1c1b-1bb2-5f2d-969b-c27a582c5eaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2a11e40-d8b5-5095-a64c-10947d9f3965" :question/math-academy-id "q-37138" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-2y,$ where $x=s{t}^{2}$ and $y={s}^{3}-t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =({s}^{3}-t)^{2}={s}^{6}-2{s}^{3}t+{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2(s{t}^{2})({s}^{3}-t)-2=2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-2s{t}^{3}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & ={t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =3{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =({s}^{6}-2{s}^{3}t+{t}^{2})({t}^{2})+(2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-2s{t}^{3}-2)(3{s}^{2}) \\\\  & ={s}^{6}{t}^{2}-2{s}^{3}{t}^{3}+{t}^{4}+6{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-6{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2} \\\\  & =7{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-8{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59029508-b3c4-5856-9e82-8be13d20dfcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321849} {:answer/id #uuid "5bfe432d-69ae-5f45-8501-b2941dd6da47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321875} {:answer/id #uuid "73b5e790-d197-591e-8023-1b42b9c19647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322050} {:answer/id #uuid "84f4df8e-914d-5611-8d98-3e64db0593e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322199} {:answer/id #uuid "a8213e0d-3524-56f9-9f0b-d587a6a113bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bfe432d-69ae-5f45-8501-b2941dd6da47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321875} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8f76a21-833b-5523-ac04-4ad00b86b928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7818e95-0cd3-5ca9-8225-4d2224300f3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38133" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}2{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}2{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f4dfaf9-6adc-58e2-af62-11084821e994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327358} {:answer/id #uuid "88ee277f-7d39-51d6-ae8e-804455cc5483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328112} {:answer/id #uuid "9908e0fd-b391-508e-8dd9-9f1b7572eaab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328256} {:answer/id #uuid "b457a22e-59a5-554f-b4d6-d662134d1479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328503} {:answer/id #uuid "d0528873-0750-5fae-828d-e2dd5c23f87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f4dfaf9-6adc-58e2-af62-11084821e994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327358} :answer-field/id #uuid "a085cb5f-e013-5736-9ee5-2fef11903a5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "770be8a8-209f-5a4a-8f5b-267370ed95fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-43288" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c09847-2ab1-5fb6-aa84-b7fd83b2bb5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327149} {:answer/id #uuid "68cf78c6-d0fb-5db7-80d9-19e5ac4e8c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327839} {:answer/id #uuid "6b1f2fd2-2e2b-561f-aee3-ea8774ddfad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327858} {:answer/id #uuid "976c80ed-cab7-5eb4-bcd2-8cfa8928b4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328245} {:answer/id #uuid "e0257c29-bec9-5ad9-85a7-a7ff4ac96f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "976c80ed-cab7-5eb4-bcd2-8cfa8928b4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328245} :answer-field/id #uuid "6982ad4e-ae8e-5626-9538-ed0986cbdc90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42f409ec-583f-5739-b6a6-cc4f7712efc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-43291" :question/problem "Simplify $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}]}
